1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,962
Welcome to Lesson Three of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction to

2
00:00:03,962 --> 00:00:07,203
Quantum Computing. Lessons One and Two established superposition and

3
00:00:07,203 --> 00:00:09,004
controllable probability on one qubit.

4
00:00:09,004 --> 00:00:14,406
Now we add a second qubit and use a controlled gate to create a Bell

5
00:00:14,406 --> 00:00:16,567
pair with perfectly correlated ideal measurements.

6
00:00:16,947 --> 00:00:18,740
The construction has three steps.

7
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:21,608
Hadamard prepares the first qubit in equal superposition.

8
00:00:21,608 --> 00:00:26,627
C not uses that qubit as the control and the second qubit as the

9
00:00:26,627 --> 00:00:29,496
target. Measurement then reveals two allowed correlated outcomes:

10
00:00:29,496 --> 00:00:31,288
zero zero and one one.

11
00:00:31,668 --> 00:00:35,839
The objective is to build a two-qubit Bell-state circuit, predict

12
00:00:35,839 --> 00:00:38,619
the allowed measurement strings, execute one thousand local

13
00:00:38,619 --> 00:00:42,095
simulator shots, and verify that every observed outcome is matched.

14
00:00:42,095 --> 00:00:46,266
We will also state clearly what this result does and does not

15
00:00:46,266 --> 00:00:46,613
establish.

16
00:00:46,993 --> 00:00:50,891
Two measured qubits can produce four classical bit strings: zero

17
00:00:50,891 --> 00:00:54,009
zero, zero one, one zero, and one one.

18
00:00:54,009 --> 00:00:57,907
The Bell circuit does not make all four equally likely.

19
00:00:57,907 --> 00:01:01,416
Its gate sequence creates a very specific correlation structure.

20
00:01:01,796 --> 00:01:05,821
In the controlled X operation, qubit zero is the control and qubit

21
00:01:05,821 --> 00:01:10,518
one is the target. The target flips only when the control is in the

22
00:01:10,518 --> 00:01:14,879
one branch. If the control is in the zero branch, the target remains

23
00:01:14,879 --> 00:01:15,215
unchanged.

24
00:01:15,595 --> 00:01:18,628
The circuit applies Hadamard to qubit zero first.

25
00:01:18,628 --> 00:01:21,282
That creates equal zero and one branches.

26
00:01:21,282 --> 00:01:25,074
Controlled X then links qubit one to the control branch.

27
00:01:25,074 --> 00:01:28,486
Finally, both qubits are measured in the computational basis.

28
00:01:28,866 --> 00:01:31,629
The ideal state is called Bell phi plus.

29
00:01:31,629 --> 00:01:36,464
It is written as zero zero plus one one, divided by the square root

30
00:01:36,464 --> 00:01:40,264
of two. This means the joint state contains the two matched

31
00:01:40,264 --> 00:01:43,026
possibilities with equal amplitude, rather than four independent

32
00:01:43,026 --> 00:01:43,372
combinations.

33
00:01:43,752 --> 00:01:47,082
The preserved source again uses one thousand shots and seed

34
00:01:47,082 --> 00:01:50,413
sixty-seven. Keeping the same simulation settings lets us compare

35
00:01:50,413 --> 00:01:53,410
this lesson directly with the earlier seeded fifty-fifty

36
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:54,410
single-qubit result.

37
00:01:54,790 --> 00:01:58,503
The builder allocates two qubits, applies Hadamard to qubit zero,

38
00:01:58,503 --> 00:02:02,588
applies controlled X from qubit zero to qubit one, and measures

39
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:06,673
both. The entire Bell-state preparation is visible in two gate

40
00:02:06,673 --> 00:02:07,044
lines.

41
00:02:07,424 --> 00:02:10,829
The main function executes the circuit, collects the two-bit

42
00:02:10,829 --> 00:02:14,574
measurement counts, and adds the zero-zero and one-one totals.

43
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:17,979
Dividing that sum by one thousand produces the matched percentage

44
00:02:17,979 --> 00:02:20,362
used as the lesson's acceptance check.

45
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:23,033
Before executing, the prediction is specific.

46
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:27,616
Zero zero and one one should each appear near half the time.

47
00:02:27,616 --> 00:02:32,962
Zero one and one zero should not appear at all in this ideal model.

48
00:02:32,962 --> 00:02:37,545
The exact split between the two allowed strings can vary with finite

49
00:02:37,545 --> 00:02:37,927
sampling.

50
00:02:38,307 --> 00:02:42,593
Now we run the preserved Lesson Three Python file in the same local

51
00:02:42,593 --> 00:02:45,891
Qiskit environment. The output prints the text circuit diagram, the

52
00:02:45,891 --> 00:02:49,518
raw two-bit counts, and the percentage of outcomes where both

53
00:02:49,518 --> 00:02:50,507
measured bits agree.

54
00:02:50,887 --> 00:02:53,630
The printed circuit confirms the intended structure.

55
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:57,940
Qubit zero passes through Hadamard, then controls the X operation on

56
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:01,466
qubit one. Both measurement boxes appear after the entangling

57
00:03:01,466 --> 00:03:05,776
operation, so the terminal output is tied to the correct circuit.

58
00:03:06,156 --> 00:03:10,120
The measured counts are five hundred seven zero-zero outcomes and

59
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:12,643
four hundred ninety-three one-one outcomes.

60
00:03:12,643 --> 00:03:15,526
Zero one and one zero do not appear.

61
00:03:15,526 --> 00:03:18,409
The allowed pair totals one thousand shots exactly.

62
00:03:18,789 --> 00:03:22,036
Adding the two allowed counts gives one thousand matched outcomes

63
00:03:22,036 --> 00:03:25,608
out of one thousand shots. The matched percentage is therefore one

64
00:03:25,608 --> 00:03:27,556
hundred percent in this ideal simulation.

65
00:03:27,556 --> 00:03:30,478
That is the direct acceptance result for the lesson.

66
00:03:30,858 --> 00:03:33,555
The chart makes the result immediately visible.

67
00:03:33,555 --> 00:03:37,792
Zero zero and one one carry all measured counts, while both

68
00:03:37,792 --> 00:03:39,719
mismatched categories remain at zero.

69
00:03:39,719 --> 00:03:43,571
The distribution is random between the two allowed results but

70
00:03:43,571 --> 00:03:45,497
perfectly constrained by their relationship.

71
00:03:45,877 --> 00:03:49,214
Each shot still produces a random choice between the two allowed

72
00:03:49,214 --> 00:03:52,550
strings. The key quantum structure is not that one fixed string

73
00:03:52,550 --> 00:03:55,887
always appears. It is that the two measurement results agree every

74
00:03:55,887 --> 00:03:57,404
time in the ideal model.

75
00:03:57,784 --> 00:04:01,733
If the two qubits were merely independent fifty-fifty random bits,

76
00:04:01,733 --> 00:04:04,964
all four strings would appear near twenty-five percent.

77
00:04:04,964 --> 00:04:08,554
The Bell circuit instead concentrates all probability on the two

78
00:04:08,554 --> 00:04:11,785
matched strings. That difference is the correlation we are

79
00:04:11,785 --> 00:04:12,144
verifying.

80
00:04:12,524 --> 00:04:16,123
The verification is direct. The raw counts total one thousand, only

81
00:04:16,123 --> 00:04:19,396
the two predicted matched strings appear, and the matched percentage

82
00:04:19,396 --> 00:04:22,995
is one hundred percent. The printed circuit also confirms that the

83
00:04:22,995 --> 00:04:25,940
measured result came from Hadamard followed by controlled X.

84
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,781
This demonstration is not by itself a Bell-inequality experiment, a

85
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:34,431
test of locality, or evidence from physical quantum hardware.

86
00:04:34,431 --> 00:04:38,081
It establishes the ideal circuit behavior: the selected gate

87
00:04:38,081 --> 00:04:40,920
sequence creates the predicted correlated measurement distribution.

88
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:45,286
On real hardware, gate error, readout error, connectivity, and

89
00:04:45,286 --> 00:04:47,500
calibration can introduce mismatched counts.

90
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:51,042
Those effects become useful subjects for later experiments.

91
00:04:51,042 --> 00:04:55,471
Here, the ideal simulator isolates the intended Bell-state circuit

92
00:04:55,471 --> 00:04:57,242
before adding device noise.

93
00:04:57,622 --> 00:05:01,693
The result is reproducible because the exact source is preserved,

94
00:05:01,693 --> 00:05:06,579
the seed and shot count are fixed, and the evidence includes the

95
00:05:06,579 --> 00:05:10,244
printed circuit, raw counts, matched calculation, and visual chart.

96
00:05:10,244 --> 00:05:13,501
Each artifact describes the same measured state path.

97
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:15,622
Lesson Three correlates two qubits.

98
00:05:15,622 --> 00:05:19,105
In Lesson Four, we extend the same pattern to three.

99
00:05:19,105 --> 00:05:23,981
One Hadamard and two controlled X gates create a G H Z state whose

100
00:05:23,981 --> 00:05:27,464
ideal measurements are zero zero zero and one one one.

101
00:05:27,844 --> 00:05:31,598
That completes Lesson Three of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction

102
00:05:31,598 --> 00:05:35,727
to Quantum Computing. We combined Hadamard and C not, measured a

103
00:05:35,727 --> 00:05:39,105
real local simulation, and verified one hundred percent matched

104
00:05:39,105 --> 00:05:42,484
outcomes. The runnable Lesson Three code is linked below.

105
00:05:42,484 --> 00:05:46,613
Follow Renaissance Field Lite and Codex 67 for the complete series.
